# .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — the entire cooperative abort model is gone
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never thrown** by the runtime

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;
    private Task _workerTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up and exit
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        // Signal cancellation (replaces _running = false)
        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with a timeout (replaces Thread.Sleep + Abort)
        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _workerTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Worker didn't stop in time — log and move on.
                // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort in .NET 8.
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **No forced kill** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort`. If `GetNextItem()` blocks on unmanaged I/O without accepting a `CancellationToken`, it **cannot be interrupted**. You must refactor blocking calls to accept tokens. |
| **`volatile bool` → `CancellationToken`** | A bare `volatile bool` can't wake a blocked thread. `CancellationToken` integrates with `async` APIs, semaphores, and `WaitHandle`. |
| **Thread → Task.Run** | `Task.Run` uses the thread pool (more efficient). Set `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning` only if the loop truly never yields. |
| **Sync → Async** | If you **can't** make `GetNextItem` async, wrap the blocking call: `await Task.Run(() => GetNextItem(token), token)` — but prefer true async. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** | This catch block becomes dead code in .NET 8. Replace with `OperationCanceledException` and guard with `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()`** | No replacement needed — cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow, so `finally` blocks run normally. |
| **Dispose the CTS** | `CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable`; leaking it leaks registrations. |

## If You Can't Make Calls Async

For legacy sync-only code that truly blocks (e.g., `Console.ReadLine`, unmanaged socket):

```csharp
// Register a callback that unblocks the resource directly
token.Register(() => _blockingResource.Close());
```

This forces the blocking call to throw, giving you a clean exit path. There is **no safe general-purpose replacement** for `Thread.Abort` — cooperative cancellation is the only supported model in .NET 8.
